WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.060
- Whether you've been aware of it or not,

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:05.500
your brain has been telling you
a story about your own life.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.120
It's been telling you a
story about who you are,

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.010
what your personality is like,

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:11.950
what your strengths and weaknesses are,

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:14.500
how likely you are to
stick to certain habits,

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.830
and how easily you give up
on taking care of yourself.

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:19.460
Maybe it's been telling you a story

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:21.690
about how success and happiness

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.220
are for other people, not you.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:27.170
You're kind of doomed to
repeat your same old mistakes.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:29.310
You're not very good
at sticking to habits.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.240
You don't really commit
to changing your life

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:32.073
for the better.

00:00:32.073 --> 00:00:33.250
Maybe the life that you thought

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:34.960
is the one that you wanted to live,

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.560
just isn't in the cards for you.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.330
Maybe your brain has been telling you

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:41.730
that you're a shy person,
or an extroverted person.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:44.830
Maybe it's been telling you
that you suck at public speaking

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:46.240
and you get way too nervous.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:47.450
And you would never do that.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.690
You could never speak in
front of thousands of people.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:52.430
Some people are extremely
aware of the fact

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:54.880
that their brain tells
them certain stories.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.680
Other people live their life
kind of oblivious to this.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.380
They just think that things
are the way that they are.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:02.790
They think the way that they
think they believe the things

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:03.623
that they believe.

00:01:03.623 --> 00:01:06.660
And this is actually kind of
a problem for a lot of people

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.710
if you remain unaware of
the fact that your brain

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:12.660
is constantly crafting a
narrative about your life,

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.930
your tendencies, the people around you,

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:16.890
and the role that you
play in the universe.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.520
And we can't escape these stories forming,

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.530
it's because our subconscious
is extremely perceptive.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:25.970
Your subconscious picks up
on basically everything.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:27.770
I've mentioned on this channel before

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:31.300
that over 90% of our
overall mental function

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.820
is subconscious.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:36.020
That's kind of the consensus
for psychologists these days.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:38.980
Bottom line is that we
are constantly observing,

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:42.210
picking up and taking note
of every sight and smell,

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:43.750
every taste and texture,

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:45.750
everything that everyone says about you,

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:48.420
either in front of your
face or behind your back,

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.470
every small success and bitter failure

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:54.560
is woven into this story
that you tell yourself

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:55.550
about yourself.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.230
And we believe this story.

00:01:57.230 --> 00:01:58.860
You know, we kind of have no choice.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:00.500
We have to pick up on patterns

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.600
otherwise the world seems too chaotic.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.010
But I think the problem is that

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:07.830
what we think happened often
isn't what actually happened.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:09.720
We are a highly emotional creatures.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.310
We interpret things with
a lot of subjectivity,

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:13.990
and a lot of these opinions were formed

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:16.570
when we were so young that
we didn't even really have

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:18.230
the ability to think rationally.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:20.570
You know, if your older
brother decides to play

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:22.620
with the cool kid on the
block instead of you,

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.460
as the younger brother, you
might feel really left out

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.300
and you might think that nobody likes you

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:28.850
or nobody cares about you.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:31.980
And if a couple more situations
like this start to happen,

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.500
it can really start to develop a belief

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.030
that you are not worth hanging out with.

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:38.570
You know, no one really likes you.

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:40.110
You're not worthy of love.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:43.750
And you'll carry that with you
subconsciously through life,

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:47.290
having no idea that that belief
is there or why it's there,

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:50.950
but it will serve as sort of
a fundamental character trait

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:52.660
woven into your story.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:55.530
And it gets even trickier
because human beings

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.380
really like to stay
consistent with this story

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.100
since it is the fundamental framework,

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:03.430
the lens in which we view the world

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:04.640
and make sense of things

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.230
so things don't seem so utterly chaotic.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.810
When new information is provided to us

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:13.150
that contradicts that
story, we tend to reject it.

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:17.220
We will throw out new
information in favor of ideas

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:20.330
that help confirm our cognitive bias,

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:23.200
things in line with the
story that we've always told.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.430
And that really sucks
because a lot of the time,

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:28.640
a lot of the time, the
stories we tell ourselves

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:29.750
don't serve us.

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.240
We might think it serves
us because we think

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.570
it's the truth because
we haven't questioned it.

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:37.630
But the proof of its
efficacy is in the pudding.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.420
You know, if you're not
where you want to be in life,

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:43.800
if you feel unsatisfied
on a day-to-day basis,

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.310
if you don't have a sense of
inner peace or satisfaction

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.590
with what you do or the state of the world

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.010
or the state of yourself,

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:53.880
there's a good chance that
your story isn't serving you.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.280
So what's the solution?

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:58.680
Well, thankfully, there's this
thing called Neuroplasticity,

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.130
which is kind of a general umbrella term

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:04.950
that refers to the brain's
ability to modify change

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:08.160
and adapt both structure
and function throughout life

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:10.190
and in response to experience.

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:11.200
And if you think about it,

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.190
this is probably the most
optimistic psychological discovery

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:18.340
in history for people who
don't really like the way

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:20.790
that they behave, or the thought patterns

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:22.540
that they tend to dive into.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:25.390
And that's because by
introducing new stimulus

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:29.270
or getting new experiences or
behaving in a different way

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:32.970
over time can actually
change the literal structure

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:36.340
of your brain so that those
changes become permanent.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:40.110
You can redesign your brain in
a way that better serves you.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:42.110
And really the only thing stopping you

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:45.490
is cognitive bias and
cognitive dissonance.

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:48.520
And the fact that to
behave in a different way,

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:51.750
in a way that isn't the way
that you've always acted,

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:53.420
it's gonna feel uncomfortable.

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:54.750
It's gonna feel unnatural.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:56.490
It's gonna feel like it's not really you

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:57.550
doing these things.

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:58.870
But I can guarantee you

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:00.850
that if you do these things long enough,

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:04.880
it's not just some sort of
overused empty platitude

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.430
that these things will
start to feel more natural.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:10.190
They will. Those things
will start to feel like you.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:12.970
By doing these things over
a long period of time,

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:15.760
your subconscious that is always watching

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:19.230
starts to actually develop
evidence that you are this way.

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:22.350
By acting in this new way,
in the present moment,

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:25.610
day after day, eventually
that present becomes the past.

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:27.950
And when your subconscious
looks over your life,

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:30.320
you have evidence from
the past and the present

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:32.710
of this new behavior in this new lifestyle

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:35.170
and it can predict and assume confidently

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:38.370
that you are capable of
continuing this lifestyle

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:41.670
and you are capable of change
going on into the future.

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:43.330
So it all starts with awareness.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:46.620
It all starts by taking a step
back and trying to figure out

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:50.310
what story your brain is
telling yourself about yourself?

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:52.680
Who are you according to you?

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:54.730
What things do you usually do?

00:05:54.730 --> 00:05:58.350
And what behaviors do
you engage in day to day

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:00.310
that reaffirms that belief?

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:02.520
And how can you modify those behaviors

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.030
to better serve you so
that when your subconscious

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:08.150
naturally does its thing
and observes your behavior,

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:09.840
it has some evidence to go off

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.560
of that you are indeed this new person,

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:14.860
you are indeed the
person that you actually

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:16.820
intentionally want to be this time,

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:19.100
rather than the person
that you kind of ended up

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:20.533
as by accident.

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:22.930
Since the beginning of time,

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:26.010
human beings have used stories
to help them make sense

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:27.130
of the world around them.

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:30.690
We are compelled by stories
when someone tells a funny joke,

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.730
or when we all flocked
to the movie theater

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:34.080
to see the new Spider-Man.

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.687
And for that reason, I
highly recommend the book,

